{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 14, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "the social network, directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, stands as a defining film of the digital age. released in 2010, it chronicles the founding of facebook and the subsequent legal battles that plagued its creator, mark zuckerberg. the movie is not just a biography but a sharp examination of ambition, betrayal, and the isolating nature of success. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as zuckerberg, portraying him not as a villain but as a socially awkward genius driven by a desire for recognition and belonging. his rapid-fire dialogue, characteristic of sorkin's writing style, creates a rhythmic intensity that propels the narrative forward. andrew garfield also shines as eduardo saverin, the co-founder whose friendship with zuckerberg disintegrates under the weight of corporate maneuvering. the film's structure, alternating between the creation of the site and the depositions years later, adds a layer of tragic irony to the story. trent reznor and atticus ross composed a haunting, electronic score that perfectly captures the cold, sterile atmosphere of the coding rooms and the emotional void at the center of the story. visually, fincher uses a muted color palette and precise camera work to emphasize the clinical precision of the technology world. despite being about computers and code, the movie is deeply human, exploring how connections made online can lead to real-world disconnection. it remains a masterpiece of modern cinema, offering a timeless critique of how we relate to one another in an increasingly connected yet lonely world.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.07889063}, {"token": " social", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " network", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.3984781}, {"token": " directed", "logprob": -2.1457695e-06}, {"token": " by", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " david", "logprob